    <!-- Architecture of a browser python code runner -->
    <div class="mb-10">
      <h4 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-semibold tracking-[-0.02em] text-[var(--g-ink)] mb-3">Architecture of a browser python code runner</h4>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-[var(--g-body)] leading-relaxed mb-4">
        A python code runner is an execution program that compiles Python source statements into bytecode and evaluates them directly inside an isolated web browser process without contacting external servers. The engine allocates 38.4 megabytes of WebAssembly heap space upon initialization in Chrome or Firefox. Execution starts immediately. Python Code Runner loads CPython 3.12 through the official Pyodide 0.26.4 distribution to ensure complete standard library compliance across every modern browser session without installing software. Memory holds your files. The Pyodide virtual file system preserves modified scripts in browser storage so that students can resume programming sessions without data loss.
      </p>
      <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-[var(--g-body)] leading-relaxed mb-4">
        Modern web browsers enforce strict security policies on WebAssembly threads, which prevents foreign scripts from inspecting local directories or compromising operating system files on Windows and macOS. This sandbox provides total safety. Whenever code triggers an infinite loop or allocates excessive memory, the browser task manager allows the programmer to terminate the web worker without crashing the browser. Safety remains guaranteed.
      </p>
    </div>

